{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 Calibri;}}\f0\fs24 Thyme Names That Sound Seasonal: Keeping Shelf Labels From Reading as a Sowing Calendar \u183? Questions and answers\par Seasonal words in thyme selection names are variety names, not sowing instructions. This guide answers the practical questions retailers and buyers ask when a packet labelled "Winter thyme" sits beside one labelled "Summerthyme" and the shelf seems to imply a sowing calendar.\par \b Do the seasonal words in thyme names indicate when to sow?\b0\par No. Within the wholesale thyme catalogue, names such as Summerthyme, Winter thyme and English Winter distinguish selections within Thymus vulgaris. They form part of the imported reference and travel with the seed article, not with a month. The name is a way of identifying a specific selection, not a timing instruction for every destination. When the word appears without context, customers reasonably read it as a schedule, which is where the confusion begins.\par \b How should a packet separate the name from the guidance?\b0\par Keep the selection name in one visual zone and any timing or cultivation guidance in another. A consistent layout carries the clarification without a long explanation. Three elements are worth showing: the selection name exactly as it appears in the reference together with its SKU or article number, the botanical identity Thymus vulgaris stated once and clearly, and separate timing guidance attributed to its source rather than implied by the name. Placing the SKU next to the name also helps staff match a returning customer's request for "the winter one" to the correct article.\par \b What wording keeps a name a name?\b0\par A single line on the packet or shelf can do the work, for example: "Winter thyme" is the selection name. It is not a sowing month. Variations are fine as long as the sentence keeps naming the selection and denies the calendar reading. Avoid replacing the name with a descriptive phrase of your own, because a renamed packet no longer matches the article the customer reorders next season.\par \b Why does the quantity basis matter here?\b0\par Seasonal confusion often sits next to a second one: what the price refers to. A price per kilogram is a different basis from a price for a stated number of seeds. Where the shelf shows a price, show the recorded unit beside it, so the selection name, the article and the quantity basis read as one unit. This keeps the shelf honest and reduces queries that have nothing to do with the seed itself.\par \b How do you train the shelf rather than just the packet?\b0\par Most misreading happens before the packet is picked up. A shelf-edge line such as "Selection names are variety names, not sowing dates" prevents the question instead of answering it repeatedly. Staff who know the seasonal word belongs to the imported reference can explain it in one sentence and can point to the SKU when a customer wants to compare two thyme lines. The same habit applies across herb ranges: compare species and cultivar before treating two names as synonyms, and keep named selections attached to their own SKU.\par \b What is gained by clear labelling?\b0\par Clear labelling protects the customer from a wasted sowing and the retailer from a return based on a misread label. The name stays attached to the correct article, the quantity basis stays visible, and the timing question is answered by guidance rather than by a word in a name. Retailers assembling a thyme line can draft packet text against the actual references in the wholesale thyme seed range, where the selections are grouped and named. For related comparisons of named selections, see the guidance on comparing similar herb lines and on structuring product pages for clarity.\par {\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "https://info0277593.wixsite.com/bulk-seeds-internati/beitrag/thyme-selection-names-vs-sowing-calendar"}{\fldrslt Thyme Names That Sound Seasonal: Keeping Shelf Labels From Reading as a Sowing Calendar}}